DevFlow is an independent, ground-up reimplementation. Its phase-loop discipline
— fresh-context subagents, wave-parallel execution, plan checking, goal-backward
verification, and durable .planning/ state — is derived in concept from GSD, as
are four later additions: exogenous abstention on non-inferable truths (the
backstop tier, where the verifier routes on a tag applied at plan time rather
than on its own judgment), the coincidental-reliance advisory, post-execution
conformance of a committed diff to its declared file scope, and the rule that a
guard which could not run must never report success:

  GSD Core (open-gsd/gsd-core) — MIT License
  Copyright (c) 2026 Open GSD
  https://github.com/open-gsd/gsd-core

No source files from GSD Core are included in DevFlow; the behavioral contracts
were reimplemented in a compressed, Claude-Code-only form.

DevFlow's specification discipline — inline [NEEDS CLARIFICATION] markers for
choices the spec never settled, a recorded Assumptions section, measurable
technology-agnostic success criteria, severity-rated cross-artifact
consistency analysis, and project principles whose violations are resolved by
changing the work rather than reinterpreting the principle — is derived in
concept from:

  Spec Kit (github/spec-kit) — MIT License
  Copyright (c) GitHub, Inc.
  https://github.com/github/spec-kit

No source files from Spec Kit are included; the concepts were reimplemented
against DevFlow's own artifacts, and its constitution became a section of
ARCHITECTURE.md rather than a separate governing file.

The /flow-oracle consultation loop — context bundles, advisory panels, session
lineage, detached runs, and the render-and-copy fallback — is derived in
concept from:

  oracle (steipete/oracle) — MIT License
  Copyright (c) Peter Steinberger
  https://github.com/steipete/oracle

No source files from oracle are included; DevFlow optionally drives the oracle
CLI/MCP server as an external tool when the user has it installed.

The adjudication discipline behind /flow-pr --adversarial — ruling each review
finding on two independent axes (is it true, and what happens about it) rather
than one; holding a refutation to the same evidence standard as the finding it
answers; requiring a deferral to name a backlog artifact that already exists;
screening each round against durable settled ground; and keeping closed rounds
immutable so a later correction cites the ruling it supersedes rather than
overwriting it — is derived in concept from:

  adversarial-review-skills (Dzazaleo/adversarial-review-skills) — MIT License
  Copyright (c) Dzazaleo
  https://github.com/Dzazaleo/adversarial-review-skills

No source files or prompt text from it are included. Its workflow is a manual
relay across four sessions; DevFlow drives the equivalent through its existing
cross-provider bridge, which dispatches a bounded read-only role to the peer
provider's CLI under an enforced sandbox, so only the judgment discipline was
taken and it was compressed into one reference and one template.

Four autonomy and review behaviors — first-class safety rails on an open-ended
agent loop (iteration, wall-clock, and no-progress detection), a human interrupt
channel that carries a question together with its enumerated choices as
machine-readable data, a multi-lens review panel run against a plan before any
code exists, and a librarian pass that refreshes codebase memory from what the
work actually changed — were prompted in concept by a publicly posted AgentOS
blueprint:

  AgentOS — Product Spec & Implementation Blueprint
  https://gist.github.com/iannuttall/8152098b5ce8e6c1a7499ee561ed93f4

That document is itself a reconstruction of a talk by Danny Postma rather than a
primary source, and it specifies a hosted control plane (ephemeral containers, an
object-store MCP, a task board, a push inbox) that DevFlow is deliberately not.
No source files or prompt text from it are included; only the policies above were
taken, and each was rebuilt against DevFlow's own file-and-prompt artifacts with
thresholds chosen for DevFlow's own unit of work.

